    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 15 for ABB AquaMaster4 electromagnetic water meters.\n\nKey data:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 (revision 15)\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4076767\n- Page total: 13\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 16 February 2026\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nNote: Page 13 lists production locations:\n- ABB Limited (UK)\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. (China)\n- ABB Inc. (USA)\n- ABB India Limited (India)\n\nThese are production locations, so I could add them as additional manufacturers. The schema says manufacturers[]. The cert says \"Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited\" on page 1. Production locations are separate. I'll include ABB Limited as manufacturer (and applicant). The production locations could arguably be added to manufacturers too, but they're explicitly \"production locations\". Hmm. The schema has manufacturers[] with name and address_lines. I think including them as manufacturers is reasonable since they produce the meter, but to be safe I might include them since they're manufacturers of the certified instrument. Actually the rule says \"Don't fabricate\" — the cert explicitly labels them as production locations. I'll include the primary one as manufacturer and maybe add the production locations as manufacturers too. Let me think... The safest approach: applicant = ABB Limited; manufacturers = [ABB Limited]. But the production locations are important info. I could add them as manufacturers with a note... schema doesn't allow notes on manufacturers. I'll add all four as manufacturers since they physically manufacture the water meter. Actually, hmm — \"production location\" is distinct from \"manufacturer\" in legal metrology. But they are all ABB entities. I'll include all four under manufacturers — this captures the info without fabricating. Actually let me reconsider: the certificate explicitly says \"Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited\" and later \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". Adding the production sites as manufacturers is a reasonable interpretation. I'll include them.\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (the R49 recommendation is \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\"). The schema example categories list includes \"Gas meter\" etc. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z, FET4*, and transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. The type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". X ∈ {1,3,5}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}.\n- module_designation: null or \"Not applicable\"? For water meters under R49, modules might apply. Not mentioned. I'll omit or set null.\n\nComponents: The instrument has flow sensor (FET4 series? Actually FET4 is the sensor — \"FET4 *\" with Y being 1,2,8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)) and transmitter/calculating device (FEW4XY.Z — actually FEW = flow electromagnetic water transmitter?). Let me think: AquaMaster4 — the FEW4 series is the transmitter? Actually in ABB AquaMaster naming: FEW4XY.Z is the electromagnetic flowmeter (water). FET4 is... hmm. \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" — FET4 might be the sensor tube. Y being \"integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\" suggests FET4 is the flow sensor and FEW4 the transmitter (calculating + indicating device). Tables 1/2 refer to \"transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\". So:\n- Flow sensor: FET4 (Y=... \"optionally sensor only\")\n- Transmitter (calculating and indicating device): FEW4XY.Z with X=1, 3, 5.\n\nX: 1 for standard, 3 for advanced, 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant.\n\nSo components:\n- role: \"Flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]\n- role: \"Transmitter (calculating and indicating device)\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], alternatives \"OR\" — they're variants. Characteristics: X=1 standard, X=3 advanced, X=5 premium with mobile comms (MC).\n\nActually wait — Table 5-8 are about \"the family of instruments\" — reduced bore vs full bore, AC powered vs battery powered. Tables 9-12 indicating device characteristics.\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013), scheme A, accuracy classes 1 and 2. Canonical id: \"R49-1\"? The instruction says id canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R 49-1, canonical would be \"R49-1\". Edition: the recommendation part... R 49-1 (2013) — edition 2013. The schema says edition (int). 2013 as int. Amendment: null. Actually there's R 49-1:2013 — I'll use edition: 2013. Hmm, edition for R60 is like 2000 edition. For R49-1, the (2013) is the edition year. I'll put edition: 2013.\n\nTest reports: 7 reports listed:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03, 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04, 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01, 2 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01, 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02, 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01, 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nRoles: The doc says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The certificate references \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\". Hmm, they're associated reports; roles: type_evaluation_report seems appropriate for all. Or \"test_report\". The doc says \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". Since the Type Evaluation Report is identified on page 1 (\"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\"), I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all.\n\nAlso documentation file: \"Documentation folder no. T11851-11\" — role: documentation_file, id: T11851-11.\n\nRevision history: revisions Initial through 15 with dates and changes. Revision as string.\n\nFootnotes: [1] from Table 4: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\" Marker \"1)\".\n\nNow characteristics.\n\nType level (whole-cert, applying to all):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value, unit null)\n- environmental_class: \"O\" (installed outdoors)\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\" (but for FEW45Y.Z: E1 with external antenna cable, E2 without — config level!) For tables 1 (FEW41/43): E2. Table 2 (FEW45): E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable, E2 without.\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature range ambient: -25°C / +55°C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water temperature class: T50 (+0.1°C / +50°C) — value string \"T50\", plus range. I'll do water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\", ...} and maybe a separate water_temperature_range {min:0.1,max:50}. Hmm, \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" is the range for class T50. I'll add characteristic water_temperature_range with {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Primary: 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Could add note about 16 bar. I'll record max_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and maybe an alternative in bar. Keep simple: value 1.6, unit MPa. Actually could do both: map 1.6 MPa = 16 bar; I'll include value 1.6 unit MPa only.\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- reverse_flow: true / \"intended to measure reverse flow\" → value: true? value must be number|{min,max}|string. Use string \"intended to measure reverse flow\" or boolean true. Schema says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. Use string.\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- accuracy_class: 1 and 2 — accuracy_classes in recommendation: [1, 2].\n\nModel level / matrix tables: The big tables 5-8 are the family characteristics by meter size × power supply × bore type, with accuracy class as a sub-row dimension. This is a big matrix. I should use matrix_tables for tables 5-8 and possibly 9-12.\n\nGiven there are 4 flow tables each with ~25-35 rows × 2 accuracy class rows, that's a LOT of data. I need to preserve it. The instructions say matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables. I'll include all four tables (5, 6, 7, 8) with full rows.\n\nTable 5: Reduced bore - AC mains powered. Columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nRows for table 5 (converting commas to dots):\n- DN40, 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nTable 6: Reduced bore - Battery/Renewable:\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN350, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN400, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN450, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN450, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN500, 1: 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n- DN500, 2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN600, 1: 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n- DN600, 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTable 7: Full bore - AC mains:\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN125, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN150, 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN150, 2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\n- DN200, 1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN200, 2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250, 2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN300, 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN350, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN400, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN450, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN500, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN600, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN700, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN700, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN750, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN800, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN900, 1: 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN900, 2: 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125\n- DN1000, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1000, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1050, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1100, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1100, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1200, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1350, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1350, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1400, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1500, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1600, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1650, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1800, 1&2: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\nTable 8: Full bore - Battery/Renewable:\n- DN40, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n- DN40, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n- DN50, 1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n- DN50, 2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n- DN100, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN150, 1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n- DN150, 2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200, 1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n- DN200, 2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN250, 1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n- DN250, 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300, 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n- DN300, 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN400, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600, 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n- DN600, 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN700, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN700, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN750, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN800, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN900, 2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN1000, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1100, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1350, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nTables 9-12: indicating device characteristics by meter size group and power/bore:\nTable 9 (reduced bore, AC): rows like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\": max 9999999, min 99999, VSI 0.0001.\nTable 10 (reduced bore, battery): rows.\nTable 11 (full bore, AC): rows (spanning page 11-12).\nTable 12 (full bore, battery): rows.\n\nI'll include these as matrix_tables too.\n\nTable 9 rows:\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 10:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 11 (full bore, AC, first part on page 11):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n(continuation on page 12, unlabeled — belongs to Table 11):\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700...DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350...DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 12:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200...DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900...DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nSoftware identification tables (3, 4): these are software versions with CRC. Include as matrix_tables? They are sub-component software. Could include as matrix_tables: \"Software identification FEW41/43Y.Z\" with columns software_part_code, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. That's a lot of rows but manageable. Let me include them, grouped by software part code. Table 3 has part codes: 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality): versions 03.00.03, 03.00.04, 03.02.00, 03.03.02, 03.03.03, 03.03.06, 03.04.00, 03.04.02 (spans pages 4-5). 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus/Logger/Pressure/VKL sensor updater): 02.00.02, 02.00.04. 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus/Pressure/VKL sensor updater): 02.00.01, 02.00.05.\n\nTable 4 (FEW45Y.Z): Measurement MCU: 01.02.00, 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.03; 4G Communication MCU: 01.02.00, 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.02, 01.05.01, 01.05.03; NB-IoT Communication MCU: 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.02, 01.03.03.\n\nI'll include software tables as matrix_tables with a \"software_part_code\" or \"mcu\" column.\n\nThis is getting big, but completeness matters. Let me decide: include matrix_tables for tables 3-12 (10 tables). That's fine.\n\nModel family: models would be by meter size and power supply and bore... The model-level axis is really meter size (DN40..DN1800) × power supply (AC vs battery) × bore (reduced vs full) × accuracy class. Rather than enumerate models, the matrix_tables capture it. But the schema wants model_family with models when cert covers >1 model variant. The models here are variants FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z and meter sizes. Hmm.\n\nI think for model_family I can list the type variants:\n- FEW41Y.Z (standard)\n- FEW43Y.Z (advanced)\n- FEW45Y.Z (premium, mobile comms)\nAnd meter size variants DN40–DN1800? That would be a huge list. The matrix_tables already encode the size dimension. I'll set model_family.models to the three transmitter variants plus FET4 sensor maybe. Actually the certified type designations: FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. Model variants could be listed as \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\". I'll do that.\n\nAlternatively, I could model meter sizes as model_level characteristics (qmax per DN etc.) but with 4 config axes (bore × power) each attribute would need config_level. That's complicated; matrix_tables is the right place. I'll populate model_level minimally? The rule says \"Lists of values ... is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries\". For this cert, the flow rates vary per DN within each config. I could add model_level entries keyed by e.g. \"DN40\" etc. But then which config? The attribute \"q3_permanent\" for DN40 differs between tables 5,6,7,8 (25, 25, 40, 40). So a flat model_level per DN is ambiguous. Better to keep the full matrix in matrix_tables and not duplicate into model_level. The R137 example did both, but that was a single table. Here I'll rely on matrix_tables and define models as the size/bore/power combinations? That's 100+ combos. Hmm.\n\nCompromise: model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\", models: the three transmitter variants (FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z) with attributes (variant: standard/advanced/premium-mobile-comms). The meter-size × config matrices live in matrix_tables. That seems most faithful without exploding.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe model ids like \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\" plus FET4 sensor designation. And attributes: x_variant: 1 standard, 3 advanced, 5 premium (MC). Also for FEW45: E1 with external antenna cable / E2 without.\n\nConfig level:\n- electromagnetic_environment by axis \"antenna_cable\" for FEW45Y.Z: {condition: \"with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}.\n\nType-level pressure loss class varies by size and bore: config_level attribute pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore_size\":\n- reduced bore, DN40 & DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\n- reduced bore, larger than DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n- full bore, DN40 & DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n- full bore, DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\n- full bore, DN250+: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n\nI can encode as config_level entries with condition strings, value being class string e.g. \"Δp 40\" plus bar value... The structured value: value could be \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" as string, or split: pressure_loss_class value \"Δp40\" and max_pressure_loss 0.40 bar. Let me do config_level with attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"size_and_bore\", values: [{condition: \"reduced_bore DN40-DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}, ...]. Simpler as strings. Or I could add attribute max_pressure_loss with numeric values in bar and conditions. I'll do both? Keep one: pressure_loss_class with string values including the bar equivalents.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity class also varies: U0/D0 except full bore DN250+: U3/D0. Config level: attribute flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore_and_size\", values: [{condition: \"all except full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U0 and D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0\"}].\n\nPower supply: Table 1 (FEW41/43): AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable DC 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V). Table 2 (FEW45): AC mains includes 4x 3.6 V batteries; DC 6–32 V including 4x 3.6 V; replaceable battery 6x 3.6 V (2.9–3.6 V).\n\nSplit into:\n- ac_supply_voltage: {min: 95, max: 240} V\n- supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n- dc_supply_voltage: {min: 6, max: 32} V\n- battery_count: 2 (FEW41/43) vs 6 (FEW45) — model level\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V per cell\n- battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V\n\nType-level vs model-level: temperature range, MAP, orientation, etc. are same in both tables → type_level. Power supply battery count differs by transmitter → model_level.\n\nAlso FEW45Y.Z electromagnetic environment E1/E2 config level (with axis on antenna cable). For FEW41/43 it's E2 flat. I'll put type_level electromagnetic_environment \"E2\" with footnote? No — better: config_level attribute electromagnetic_environment axis \"transmitter_and_antenna_cable\": values: {condition: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}. That captures it. Alternatively type_level E2 plus config for FEW45. I'll use config_level covering all.\n\nHmm, but simpler: type_level electromagnetic_environment value \"E2\" (applies broadly) — no, E1 applies to FEW45 with antenna cable. I'll do the config_level approach and skip type_level for this attribute.\n\nTemperature range ambient: -25/+55 °C type level. Water temp T50 class, range 0.1–50 °C.\n\nAccuracy class: 1 and 2 → recommendation.accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Also maybe characteristics type_level accuracy_class [\"1\",\"2\"]? The recommendation block has accuracy_classes; skip in characteristics.\n\nMeter size: the range of sizes: DN40 to DN1800 (reduced bore up to DN600; full bore up to DN1800). Could note in description.\n\ncertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — include revision? Number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision_history covers rev 15. Maybe number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", and there's no revision field at cert level in schema; revision_history has entries. Good.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4076767\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"16 February 2026\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" (also styled \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null — not given. Certification Board? The signature line shows \"Certification Board\" maybe as title. I'll omit or set null. Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit person_title.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\ncategory: \"Water meter\".\n\ntype_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]? Let me be careful: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". Tables mention \"transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\". So type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm \"FET4 *\" — I'll write \"FET4*\". \n\ndescription: the X/Y/Z code explanation: \"* With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nmodule_designation: null — omit.\n\ncomponents:\n1. role: \"Flow sensor (meter)\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"], characteristics: {bore options: reduced (R), virtual full bore (V), full bore (F); integral/remote Y=1,2,8,9, optionally sensor only}\n2. role: \"Transmitter / calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {\"X=1\": \"standard\", \"X=3\": \"advanced\", \"X=5\": \"premium (mobile comms - MC)\"}\n\nActually should alternatives be \"OR\"? The transmitter alternatives: yes, the cert covers FEW41 or FEW43 or FEW45. I'll set alternatives \"OR\" for transmitter component.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O\", footnote none} — maybe add note \"installed outdoors\" in value string: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Keep \"O\" and add a separate? I'll use value \"O\" with a companion? Simpler: value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm canonical: I'll do value \"O\" and add characteristic environmental_class_note? Overkill. Use value \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string. Fine.\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — the T50 class is +0.1°C to +50°C (cold water class). Actually T50 means up to 50°C; the +0.1 lower bound from the table. I'll include it.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and maybe secondary: map_pressure: {value: 16, unit: \"bar\"}? I'll add one attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with value 1.6 MPa. Could also include equivalent bar in a string. I'll add second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? That's redundant. Skip; keep MPa. Hmm, but the parenthetical (16 bar) is useful. I'll leave it as one entry with unit MPa.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- ratio_q2_q1: {value: 1.6} (from the note: ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6)\n- ratio_q4_q3: {value: 1.25}\n- minimum_ratio_q3_q1: {value: 40}\n\ncharacteristics.config_level:\n1. attribute: electromagnetic_environment, axis: transmitter_and_antenna_configuration:\n   - {condition: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}\n2. attribute: flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: bore_and_size:\n   - {condition: \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n3. attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore_and_size:\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n\nWait, for values in config_level — should they be structured {value, unit_symbol}? The config example: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". The pressure loss value includes bar in parens; since it's a class designation \"Δp 40\" with equivalent 0.40 bar, I can express value as object {value: \"Δp 40\", unit...}? Hmm. I'll express config values as structured objects: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. For pressure loss class: value \"Δp 40\", unit null, and maybe add separate max_pressure_loss config entries numeric. Let me add:\n- attribute: max_pressure_loss, axis: bore_and_size, values: [{condition: \"reduced bore DN40-DN50\", value: 0.40, unit: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore > DN50\", value: 0.63, unit: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN40-DN50\", value: 0.10, unit: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN65-DN200\", value: 0.16, unit: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250+\", value: 0.10, unit: \"bar\"}]\nAnd pressure_loss_class with \"Δp 40\" strings etc. Both are useful; I'll include both for completeness. Hmm, that may be duplicative but okay. Actually to reduce noise, I'll combine into pressure_loss_class entries with value \"Δp 40\" and add the bar value in a parallel attribute. Let me just do both attributes.\n\nAlso power supply differences between FEW41/43 and FEW45: model_level:\n- battery_cell_count: FEW41/43: 2; FEW45: 6\n- battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V (both)\n- battery_operating_range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V (both)\n\nBut battery count also depends on power mode for FEW45? \"AC mains: 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries. Renewable: 6–32 V including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries. Replaceable Battery: 6x 3,6 V\". So FEW45: 4 batteries backup in mains/DC mode, 6 in battery-only mode. FEW41/43: \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V\". So config-level with axis power_supply_mode and model. This gets complicated. I'll encode:\n\nmodel_level:\n- attribute: battery_pack_configuration, unit null, values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V); 4x 3.6 V backup in AC/DC powered mode\"}] — string values. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively:\n- replaceable_battery_cells: FEW41/43: \"2x 3.6 V\"; FEW45: \"6x 3.6 V (4x 3.6 V backup when AC/DC powered)\"\nKeep as strings since mixed.\n\nType-level power supply characteristics:\n- ac_mains_voltage: {min 95, max 240} V\n- supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz\n- dc_supply_voltage (renewable energy): {min 6, max 32} V\n- battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V\n\nmodel_level:\n- attribute: replaceable_battery_configuration, values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6x 3.6 V\"}] with footnote about FEW45 including 4x backup in powered modes? I'll add remark in value string for FEW45: \"6x 3.6 V (AC/DC modes include 4x 3.6 V backup)\". Simpler: value \"6x 3.6 V\" and a separate string? Keep \"6x 3.6 V\". And note in description? The tables capture detail. I'll do value strings.\n\nmodel_level for transmitter variant attribute:\n- attribute: transmitter_variant, values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"standard\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"advanced\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"premium (mobile Comms - MC)\"}]\n\nHmm, that's meta. Maybe skip; components section covers it. I'll keep components covering variant descriptions.\n\nmatrix_tables: 12 tables total (1,2 general characteristics — these are key-value tables; I've extracted them into characteristics, so maybe include tables 1 and 2 as matrix_tables? They're not really matrices. I think extraction into type_level/config_level/model_level suffices; but preserving tables 1&2 as matrix_tables would duplicate. I'll skip 1 and 2 in matrix_tables since fully captured.\n\nTables 3 and 4: software identification → matrix_tables.\nTables 5–8: flow rates → matrix_tables.\nTables 9–12: indicating device → matrix_tables.\n\nThat's 10 matrix_tables. Each with name, columns, rows.\n\nFor table 3 software: columns: software_part_code, software_function, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. Rows (16 entries):\nPart code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality):\n- 03.00.03 / B26AFE6B / -\n- 03.00.04 / 6E98963A / -\n- 03.02.00 / E12E5C04 / -\n- 03.03.02 / D4D1E40B / \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.03 / CEEF45F1 / \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.06 / DAC51328 / -\n- 03.04.00 / 277BA336 / -\n- 03.04.02 / 66624063 / -\nPart code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.02 / 84EBD1C8 / -\n- 02.00.04 / 5D2D2DDD / -\nPart code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.01 / C28EAA16 / -\n- 02.00.05 / F517559F / -\n\nTable 4 (FEW45Y.Z, footnote 1): columns: software_group (mcu), software_version, crc_checksum, remarks? The remarks are merged \"Software identification for Measurement MCU\" etc. Rows:\nMeasurement MCU:\n- 01.02.00 / 94E6FAE9\n- 01.03.00 / A6C913AF\n- 01.03.01 / 2BB4AE79\n- 01.03.03 / 2358C8FF\n4G Communication MCU:\n- 01.02.00 / AE3D9862\n- 01.03.00 / 3B33D2C8\n- 01.03.01 / FDE7CBB6\n- 01.03.02 / CD0C1881\n- 01.05.01 / 947F95FD\n- 01.05.03 / EC5B9ED3\nNB-IoT Communication MCU:\n- 01.03.00 / E5742163\n- 01.03.01 / EC4FF9C6\n- 01.03.02 / C8CABC2D\n- 01.03.03 / 31459473\n\nFootnote 1 on table 4 → matrix_tables.footnotes or global footnotes. Both: global footnotes[] gets marker \"1)\" text; table 4 gets footnotes [\"1)\"].\n\nValues for software versions: keep as strings (\"03.00.03\"). CRC as string.\n\nFor flow tables: numeric values; columns meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1. Units m³/h. Include column_units? The R137 example used column_units. Schema lists columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[]. Example includes column_units. I'll add column_units arrays.\n\nAccuracy class values: for \"1 & 2\" rows, I could use string \"1 & 2\" or [1,2]. Use string \"1 and 2\"? I'll use string \"1 & 2\" as-is. Hmm, or numeric list. JSON row object: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"accuracy_class\": \"1 & 2\", \"q1_minimum\": 0.05, ...}. Strings fine.\n\nFor indicating device tables: columns meter_sizes (list or string), indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max; units: m³. meter_size as string \"DN40, DN50, DN65\" or array [\"DN40\",\"DN50\",\"DN65\"]. I'll use array of strings for clarity. Rows with numeric values 9999999, 99999, 0.0001.\n\nNow the revision history: 16 entries. Revision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\"...\"15\". Dates: \"14 August 2020\" etc. changes strings.\n\nNote revision 11 date \"27 January 2024\" — as given (even though seems out of order vs rev 10 26 Feb 2024; keep as-is).\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\" — hmm canonical example \"R60\" for R 60. For R 49-1 → \"R49-1\". \n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null — omit.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n- title: \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\" — schema doesn't list title but extra field probably fine? Rule: don't fabricate; adding title is present in doc. The schema doesn't include title. I'll add it anyway? Better to stick to schema. I'll omit title to be safe. Actually including extra info can help; but instructions say follow schema. I'll omit.\n\ntest_reports: 7 entries + documentation file:\n- id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"14 August 2020\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2254799-03\", \"30 September 2020\", 44, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2254799-04\", \"24 December 2020\", 180, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2564429-01\", \"2 August 2021\", 26, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2496280-01\", \"26 August 2021\", 29, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2496280-02\", \"23 November 2021\", 25, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- \"NMi-2563125-01\", \"22 September 2023\", 94, \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- documentation: id \"T11851-11\", role: \"documentation_file\" — date/pages not given, omit. Role: documentation_file. Description says \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-11.\"\n\nHmm, are these type evaluation reports or test reports? The certificate says \"associated reports\" that established conformity; page 1 references \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". The reports listed are likely the type evaluation report(s). I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all. That's a judgment call. Fine.\n\nFootnotes:\n- marker \"1)\", text: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\nNow model_family:\nfamily_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\nmodels: [\n  {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", attributes: {variant: \"standard\"}},\n  {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", attributes: {variant: \"advanced\"}},\n  {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", attributes: {variant: \"premium (mobile Comms - MC)\"}}\n]\nMaybe also include flow sensor FET4. It's a component. Models list should probably be the meter variants. I'll keep the three transmitter-based variants. Also mention meter sizes in family? I could add a model entry per size but that's 30+ sizes × configs. Skip.\n\nShould model_level include flow-rate-by-size data? I decided matrix_tables carry it. But the guidance emphasizes model_level for attribute × model. Given ambiguity of models here (sizes vs transmitter variants), matrix_tables is the cleanest. I'll include model_level only for the battery configuration and electromagnetic... no, EM env is config_level. Model_level: replaceable_battery_configuration per transmitter model. OK.\n\nLet me now also double check power supply parsing for table 1: \"AC mains: 95 –240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" → voltage 95–240 V, frequency 50/60 Hz. \"Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6 – 32 V\". \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\".\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- ac_supply_voltage: {min: 95, max: 240} V\n- ac_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n- dc_supply_voltage: {min: 6, max: 32} V\n- battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V\n- replaceable_battery_cells (model_level): FEW41/43: 2; FEW45: 6. Actually count differs; FEW45 has 6 (or 4 backup in powered modes). Model_level numeric: {model: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: 2}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: 6} with unit \"cells\"? unit_symbol: null. Attribute: replaceable_battery_cell_count. Value 2 / 6 (numbers). Add remark? The schema allows only value/unit/footnote. Fine.\n\nAlso flow direction: reverse flow.\n\nAlso \"Temperature range ambient -25 °C / +55 °C (for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\" → ambient_temperature_range {min:-25, max:55} °C.\n\nNow assemble matrix_tables rows carefully. This is the bulk. Let me write them.\n\nMatrix table 5 rows (24 rows):\nDN40 1: 0.05/0.08/25/31.25/500\nDN40 2: 0.025/0.04/25/31.25/1000\nDN50 1: 0.08/0.128/40/50/500\nDN50 2: 0.04/0.064/40/50/1000\nDN65 1&2: 0.063/0.1008/63/78.75/1000\nDN80 1&2: 0.1/0.16/100/125/1000\nDN100 1: 0.32/0.512/160/200/500\nDN100 2: 0.16/0.256/160/200/1000\nDN125 1: 0.32/0.512/160/200/500\nDN125 2: 0.16/0.256/160/200/1000\nDN150 1&2: 0.4/0.64/400/500/1000\nDN200 1&2: 0.63/1.008/630/787.5/1000\nDN250 1: 2/3.2/1000/1250/500\nDN250 2: 1/1.6/1000/1250/1000\nDN300 1: 3.2/5.12/1600/2000/500\nDN300 2: 1.6/2.56/1600/2000/1000\nDN350 1: 3.2/5.12/1600/2000/500\nDN350 2: 1.6/2.56/1600/2000/1000\nDN400 1&2: 5/8/2500/3125/500\nDN450 1&2: 5/8/2500/3125/500\nDN500 1: 8/12.8/4000/5000/500\nDN500 2: 4/6.4/4000/5000/1000\nDN600 1: 12.6/20.16/6300/7875/500\nDN600 2: 6.3/10.08/6300/7875/1000\n\nTable 6 rows (26):\nDN40 1&2: 0.0625/0.1/25/31.25/400\nDN50 1&2: 0.1/0.16/40/50/400\nDN65 1: 0.39375/0.63/63/78.75/160\nDN65 2: 0.1575/0.252/63/78.75/400\nDN80 1: 0.625/1/100/125/160\nDN80 2: 0.25/0.4/100/125/400\nDN100 1: 1/1.6/160/200/160\nDN100 2: 0.4/0.64/160/200/400\nDN125 1: 1/1.6/160/200/160\nDN125 2: 0.4/0.64/160/200/400\nDN150 1&2: 1/1.6/400/500/400\nDN200 1&2: 1.575/2.52/630/787.5/400\nDN250 1: 6.25/10/1000/1250/160\nDN250 2: 2.5/4/1000/1250/400\nDN300 1: 10/16/1600/2000/160\nDN300 2: 4/6.4/1600/2000/400\nDN350 1: 10/16/1600/2000/160\nDN350 2: 4/6.4/1600/2000/400\nDN400 1: 15.625/25/2500/3125/160\nDN400 2: 12.5/20/2500/3125/200\nDN450 1: 15.625/25/2500/3125/160\nDN450 2: 12.5/20/2500/3125/200\nDN500 1: 100/160/4000/5000/40\nDN500 2: 40/64/4000/5000/100\nDN600 1: 157.5/252/6300/7875/40\nDN600 2: 63/100.8/6300/7875/100\n\nTable 7 rows (46):\nDN40 1&2: 0.08/0.128/40/50/500\nDN50 1&2: 0.126/0.2016/63/78.75/500\nDN65 1&2: 0.2/0.32/100/125/500\nDN80 1&2: 0.32/0.512/160/200/500\nDN100 1&2: 0.5/0.8/250/312.5/500\nDN125 1&2: 0.5/0.8/250/312.5/500\nDN150 1: 2.52/4.032/630/787.5/250\nDN150 2: 1.26/2.016/630/787.5/500\nDN200 1: 4/6.4/1000/1250/250\nDN200 2: 2/3.2/1000/1250/500\nDN250 1: 6.4/10.24/1600/2000/250\nDN250 2: 3.2/5.12/1600/2000/500\nDN300 1: 10/16/2500/3125/250\nDN300 2: 5/8/2500/3125/500\nDN350 1&2: 40/64/4000/5000/100\nDN400 1&2: 40/64/4000/5000/100\nDN450 1&2: 63/100.8/6300/7875/100\nDN500 1&2: 63/100.8/6300/7875/100\nDN600 1&2: 63/100.8/6300/7875/100\nDN700 1: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN700 2: 50/80/10000/12500/200\nDN750 1: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN750 2: 50/80/10000/12500/200\nDN800 1: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN800 2: 50/80/10000/12500/200\nDN900 1: 158.7/253.9/10000/12500/63\nDN900 2: 80/128/10000/12500/125\nDN1000 1: 160/256/16000/20000/100\nDN1000 2: 80/128/16000/20000/200\nDN1050 1: 160/256/16000/20000/100\nDN1050 2: 80/128/16000/20000/200\nDN1100 1: 253.9/406.2/16000/20000/63\nDN1100 2: 128/204.8/16000/20000/125\nDN1200 1: 253.9/406.2/16000/20000/63\nDN1200 2: 128/204.8/16000/20000/125\nDN1350 1: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1350 2: 200/320/16000/20000/80\nDN1400 1: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1400 2: 200/320/16000/20000/80\nDN1500 1: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1500 2: 200/320/16000/20000/80\nDN1600 1: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1600 2: 200/320/16000/20000/80\nDN1650 1: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1650 2: 200/320/16000/20000/80\nDN1800 1&2: 317.5/507.9/16000/20000/50\n\nTable 8 rows (38):\nDN40 1: 0.32/0.512/40/50/125\nDN40 2: 0.16/0.256/40/50/250\nDN50 1: 0.504/0.8064/63/78.75/125\nDN50 2: 0.252/0.4032/63/78.75/250\nDN65 1&2: 0.4/0.64/100/125/250\nDN80 1&2: 0.64/1.024/160/200/250\nDN100 1&2: 1/1.6/250/312.5/250\nDN125 1&2: 1/1.6/250/312.5/250\nDN150 1: 5.04/8.064/630/787.5/125\nDN150 2: 2.52/4.032/630/787.5/250\nDN200 1: 8/12.8/1000/1250/125\nDN200 2: 4/6.4/1000/1250/250\nDN250 1: 12.8/20.48/1600/2000/125\nDN250 2: 6.4/10.24/1600/2000/250\nDN300 1: 20/32/2500/3125/125\nDN300 2: 10/16/2500/3125/250\nDN350 1&2: 16/25.6/4000/5000/250\nDN400 1&2: 16/25.6/4000/5000/250\nDN450 1&2: 25.2/40.32/6300/7875/250\nDN500 1&2: 25.2/40.32/6300/7875/250\nDN600 1: 78.75/126/6300/7875/80\nDN600 2: 39.375/63/6300/7875/160\nDN700 1: 200/320/10000/12500/50\nDN700 2: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN750 1: 200/320/10000/12500/50\nDN750 2: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN800 1: 200/320/10000/12500/50\nDN800 2: 100/160/10000/12500/100\nDN900 2: 158.7/254/10000/12500/63\nDN1000 2: 160/250/16000/20000/100\nDN1050 2: 160/250/16000/20000/100\nDN1100 2: 254/406.3/16000/20000/63\nDN1200 2: 254/406.3/16000/20000/63\nDN1350 2: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1400 2: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1500 2: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1600 2: 400/640/16000/20000/40\nDN1650 2: 400/640/16000/20000/40\n\nAlso add the note about freely chosen flow rates as description/footnotes for these tables. I'll add a \"description\" to each flow table: \"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided Q3 and Q3/Q1 are from OIML R49-1:2013 §4.1; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 at least 40.\" Good — include in description of each of tables 5-8.\n\nIndicating device tables:\nTable 9 rows (5):\n[\"DN40\",\"DN50\",\"DN65\"]: max 9999999, min 99999, VSI 0.0001\n[\"DN80\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n[\"DN100\",\"DN125\",\"DN150\",\"DN200\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n[\"DN250\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n[\"DN300\",\"DN350\",\"DN400\",\"DN450\",\"DN500\",\"DN600\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 10 rows (6):\n[\"DN40\",\"DN50\"]: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n[\"DN65\"]: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n[\"DN80\",\"DN100\",\"DN125\",\"DN150\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n[\"DN200\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n[\"DN250\",\"DN300\",\"DN350\",\"DN400\",\"DN450\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n[\"DN500\",\"DN600\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 11 rows (7, combining page 11 and the unlabeled continuation on page 12):\n[\"DN40\",\"DN50\"]: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n[\"DN65\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n[\"DN80\",\"DN100\",\"DN125\",\"DN150\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n[\"DN200\",\"DN250\",\"DN300\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n[\"DN350\",\"DN400\",\"DN450\",\"DN500\",\"DN600\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n[\"DN700\",\"DN750\",\"DN800\",\"DN900\",\"DN1000\",\"DN1050\",\"DN1100\",\"DN1200\"]: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n[\"DN1350\",\"DN1400\",\"DN1500\",\"DN1600\",\"DN1650\",\"DN1800\"]: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 12 rows (7):\n[\"DN40\",\"DN50\"]: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n[\"DN65\",\"DN80\",\"DN100\",\"DN125\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n[\"DN150\"]: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n[\"DN200\",\"DN250\",\"DN300\",\"DN350\",\"DN400\",\"DN450\",\"DN500\"]: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n[\"DN600\"]: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n[\"DN700\",\"DN750\",\"DN800\"]: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n[\"DN900\",\"DN1000\",\"DN1050\",\"DN1100\",\"DN1200\",\"DN1350\",\"DN1400\",\"DN1500\",\"DN1600\",\"DN1650\"]: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nOK. Now components — the flow sensor FET4: characteristics could note \"optionally sensor only\" (Y). Also Z: R/V/F bore variants.\n\nLet me also think about \"FET4 *\" - actually in ABB AquaMaster, the sensor is called \"FET4\" (flow electromagnetic tube?) and transmitter FEW4. The type designation combines. I'll define components:\n1. role: \"Flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"], characteristics: null or omit; maybe attributes: {versions: \"Y = 1, 2, 8, 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\", bore: \"Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"}. Hmm characteristics{} shape unspecified — schema says characteristics{} free. I'll add simple key-value strings.\n2. role: \"Transmitter (calculating and indicating device)\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {x_code: \"1 = standard, 3 = advanced, 5 = premium (mobile Comms - MC)\"}.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"16 February 2026\" (matches rev 15 date and signature date).\n\nAlso \"PRODUCTS RvA C 681\" — NMi accreditation number; not in schema; skip.\n\nNow, regarding \"member_state: The Netherlands\".\n\nLet me also include in certified_type.description: the full X/Y/Z explanation.\n\nEdge: characteristics.type_level values must be structured objects. E.g. measuring_principle: {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}. Should I include unit_symbol/unit_id keys on string values? The rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers shape. I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id: null consistently, and footnote_markers only when relevant.\n\nConfig-level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol} — include unit_symbol where numeric; for strings, unit_symbol: null. Maybe also unit_id: null. Keep to condition/value/unit_symbol (+footnote_markers if needed). Fine.\n\nModel-level: {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values\": [{\"model\", \"value\"}]} — values entries have value directly numeric/string per example. The R137 example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no structured wrapper in values entries. OK follow that.\n\nNow the top-level \"certificate.number\": include revision? I'll put number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". revision_history covers \"15\". Maybe also add \"revision\": \"15\"? Not in schema. revision_history includes it. Fine.\n\nLet me now also decide whether to add \"product\" category \"Water meter\" — yes.\n\nType designations: include \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", plus explicit \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\". I'll list: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"].\n\nmanufacturers: include ABB Limited plus the three other production locations? Production locations are where the water meter is produced — functionally manufacturers. I'll include all four, since schema has no \"production_sites\" field and these are the manufacturing entities. This preserves info. Good.\n\nNow build the JSON. Order: certificate, issuing_authority, applicants, manufacturers, certified_type, model_family, components, characteristics, matrix_tables, recommendation, test_reports, revision_history, footnotes.\n\nCharacteristics type_level list:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"? recommendation has accuracy_classes; also tables state class 1 and 2. I'll skip in type_level (covered by recommendation).\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min:0.1, max:50} °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"\n- checking_facility: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- ac_supply_voltage: {min:95, max:240} V\n- ac_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n- dc_supply_voltage: {min:6, max:32} V\n- battery_cell_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6} V\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless) — from note \"ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6\"\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- minimum_ratio_q3_q1: 40\n\nconfig_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment (axis: transmitter_antenna_configuration): 3 entries as above.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (axis: bore_and_size): 2 entries.\n- pressure_loss_class (axis: bore_and_size): 5 entries.\n- max_pressure_loss (axis: bore_and_size): 5 entries with numeric bar values.\n\nmodel_level:\n- replaceable_battery_cell_count: FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6 — values per model. Wait models list in model_level uses model ids; use \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\". Unit null. Hmm FEW45's battery config: \"Replaceable Battery: 6x 3,6 V\" plus backup 4x in AC/DC. I'll set 6 with footnote? There's no footnote marker in original; I'll add remark via attribute string? Keep numeric 6 and rely on table info. Actually maybe better attribute \"replaceable_battery_configuration\" string: \"2x 3.6 V\" vs \"6x 3.6 V\". Strings capture \"2x\"/\"6x\" better. I'll use string values \"2x 3.6 V\" and \"6x 3.6 V\". \n\nAlso for FEW45 there's note about 4x 3.6 V backup batteries in AC/DC modes. Add second model_level attribute? \"backup_battery_configuration (AC/DC powered modes)\": FEW45: \"4x 3.6 V\". Others: not applicable. Could add only FEW45 entry. I'll add attribute backup_battery_configuration with values [{model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"4x 3.6 V\"}]. Fine.\n\nNow matrix_tables definitions:\n\n1. name: \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", columns: [\"software_part_code\", \"software_function\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"], rows 12, description: \"Software versions and CRC checksums for transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z.\", footnotes: [].\n\nRows:\n{software_part_code: \"3KXF208402U0113\", software_function: \"Full functionality\", software_version: \"03.00.03\", crc_checksum: \"B26AFE6B\", remarks: null}\n... etc. For \"remarks\": \"-\" use null.\n\n2. name: \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\", columns: [\"software_group\", \"software_mcu\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows 14, footnotes: [\"1)\"].\nRows with software_group: \"Measurement\", software_mcu: \"Measurement MCU\"; \"4G\", \"Communication MCU\"; \"NB-IoT\", \"Communication MCU\".\n\n3-6. Flow tables.\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\n\nNames: \"Table 5: General characteristics of the family of instruments — reduced bore, AC mains powered\" etc. I'll write names without \"Table N\" prefix? Keep close to original: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (reduced bore, AC mains powered)\". Fine.\n\n7-10. Indicating device tables.\ncolumns: [\"meter_sizes\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"]\n\nNow revision_history entries: revision strings \"Initial\",\"1\",...,\"15\". Dates and changes as given.\n\nFootnotes: marker \"1)\" text as given. Note the footnote marker in the markdown appears as <sup>[1]</sup>; I'll use \"1)\" as marker? The footnote text begins \"**[1]**\". Marker: \"[1]\"? Use \"1)\". Rules example uses \"1)\". I'll use \"1)\".\n\nEverything as one JSON. Let me carefully compose, watching JSON validity (no trailing commas).\n\nAlso add \"documentation\" test report entry: {\"id\": \"T11851-11\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"} — pages/date omitted.\n\nDouble-check date_issued: The certificate signing shows \"16 February 2026\" next to Certification Board, and rev 15 date is 16 February 2026. Good.\n\nPerson responsible \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — keep as-is.\n\nOIML issuer id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and number ...-NL1-...).\n\nNow write final JSON. I need to be very careful with syntax. Also, ensure decimal numbers like 0.0001 are fine.\n\nOne more consideration: characteristics.model_level battery config uses models matching model_family models ids. Good.\n\nmatrix_tables for flow tables: I should include a \"description\" mentioning the flow-rate selection conditions. And maybe footnotes: none.\n\nLet me write out the JSON now.\n\nAlso I want to double check table 7 first rows: \"DN40 1&2: 0,08 / 0,128 / 40 / 50 / 500\" yes. DN50: 0.126/0.2016/63/78.75/500 yes.\n\nAnd table 8 DN900 row: class 2: 158.7/254/10000/12500/63 — note q2 254 (not 253.9). Keep as 254. DN1100 2: 254/406.3. Yes table 8 uses 406.3 while table 7 uses 406.2. Keep as-is.\n\nRatio col in table 8 DN900: 63. Yes.\n\nNow the config_level \"max_pressure_loss\": unit \"bar\". Values 0.40, 0.63, 0.10, 0.16, 0.10.\n\npressure_loss_class values: \"Δp 40\", \"Δp 63\", \"Δp 10\", \"Δp 16\", \"Δp 10\" with unit null. Include the bar equivalents via the other attribute.\n\nConditions:\n- \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\"\n- \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\"\n- \"(virtual) full bore, DN40 and DN50\"\n- \"(virtual) full bore, DN65 to DN200\"\n- \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\"\n\nOK, writing final JSON now.\n\nAlso include \"accuracy_class\" type_level? I'll include: {\"value\": \"1 and 2\", ...} to be thorough. Sure.\n\nCertificate-level: page_total 13.\n\nFinal check on rule 2: values structured with unit_symbol and unit_id everywhere in type_level. I'll include unit_id: null everywhere, unit_symbol null for strings.\n\nWrite it.",
